# Accepted undeclared module-level imports (issue #1041).
#
# One line per (path, import name): `allow <path> <import-name> <issue>`.
# Whole-line comments only - `#` opens a comment and also opens every issue
# reference on a record, which is the lesson `.bandit-audit-allow` records.
#
# THIS LEDGER IS TWO-SIDED. An entry that accounts for nothing is a finding
# (`UNDECLARED-IMPORT-STALE`, exit 1), so a line cannot outlive the import it
# records and rot into a permanent blindfold. That is the property a blanket
# skip has no way to express.
#
# An entry here says "this file is deliberately run outside the project
# environment, and its import is therefore not owed a declaration". It is NOT a
# place to park an import that should be declared.
#
# IT IS EMPTY, AND THE EMPTINESS WAS EARNED RATHER THAN DEFAULTED. #1041 opened
# with one intended entry - `woodpecker/bootstrap-secrets.py` importing boto3,
# genuine host tooling run under the system interpreter. Declaring the six
# guarded `lib/` integrations as extras in the same change put boto3 in
# `pyproject.toml`, which resolved that import too, and the stale direction above
# reported the entry on its first run: the record would have been false the
# moment it was written. Declaring the package beat recording an exception, and
# the ledger is what said so.
#
# So: prefer a declaration. Reach for a line here only when the file genuinely
# does not run from this project's environment AND the package genuinely does not
# belong in its metadata.
